WHY THIS WORKS
- never uses her fear of time as leverage, which is the easiest sale available here
- says plainly that age matters, then refuses to treat a birthday as a cliff
- arc 2 tells her she does not need the program, on the exact turn she is most likely to buy

CONVERSATION 1: 39, fourteen months, terrified of turning 40

Lead: I turn 40 in March and I can't stop thinking about it. We've been trying 14 months and I feel like the door is closing while I sit on a waiting list.

Sonia: Fourteen months of waiting with a birthday coming at you is its own kind of pressure, separate from anything biological.

I won't tell you age doesn't matter, because it does. I also won't tell you a birthday changes something overnight, because it doesn't.

Lead: everyone talks about 40 like it's a cliff.

Sonia: It gets talked about that way because it's a round number. What actually happens is gradual, and it's the same in February as it is in March.

What's been done in the fourteen months?

Lead: Bloods, a scan, and my partner did a sample. All normal. Now we're on a list.

Sonia: Then the list is what's making the time feel wasted, and it's the one part of this you can't speed up.

Those months aren't empty though. That's the window I'd want to use.

Lead: Use for what? Nobody has suggested anything except waiting.

Sonia: Working out what in your case is actually worth changing, and then changing it while you wait, so you arrive at that appointment in a different state than you left the last one.

I can't tell you what that is from a message. I can tell you nobody has looked.

Lead: That's the first useful thing anyone has said about the waiting.

Sonia: Is having a baby the thing everything else is currently arranged around?

Lead: Completely. It's all I think about.

Sonia: Then let's not spend the wait doing nothing. My team will go through the fourteen months on a call, and it's paid coaching, which I'd rather you heard from me now.

Lead: Understood. I'd like to book.

Sonia: Both of you if he can make it.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: 36, one month of trying, frightened by a video

Lead: I'm 36 and we started trying last month. I watched something about egg quality falling off after 35 and I've barely slept since.

Sonia: Then that video did its job on you and not much else.

Thirty-five isn't a switch. It shifts gradually across a decade, and one month of trying at 36 is not a warning sign.

Lead: so I'm not already too late?

Sonia: Nothing about one month says late. Most healthy couples take up to a year, and that holds at 36.

Is there anything else in the picture, though? Irregular cycles, something a doctor has flagged.

Lead: No, nothing. Cycles are regular. I'm just frightened.

Sonia: Then the fear is the thing to deal with, not your biology.

You don't need what I do yet, and I'd rather say so than take advantage of a bad night's sleep.

Lead: I half expected you to tell me to hurry.

Sonia: Manufacturing panic would get you to book. It wouldn't get you pregnant.

Watch my masterclass when you have a spare hour. It'll replace what you got from that video with something you can actually use.

{{masterclass_link}}

Lead: Thank you. That's a relief.

Sonia: Come back if something changes, or in a year if nothing has.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: At 39 every single month counts, so I wouldn't wait on this. The women who start early are the ones who get results. Shall I send you the link?

It converts her fear of time straight into a booking, which is the pressure tactic the manual rules out, and implies an outcome nobody can promise.
